<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bogislaw XIV</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1580">1580</a>–<a href="/years/1637">1637</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-griffins">House of Griffins</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bogislaw XIV was the last Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-griffins">House of Griffins</a>, ruling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/szczecin">Stettin</a> from 1620 and all Pomerania from 1625. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> occupied the duchy from 1630. His death without heirs in 1637 led to the division of Pomerania between Sweden and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-westphalia">Peace of Westphalia</a> in 1648.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bogislaw-xiv.jpg" alt="Bogislaw XIV" width="320" height="490" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Barnim_VI_-_Bogus%C5%82aw_XIV_(ca_1635).tif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Bogislaw was born in Barth on 31 March 1580, the third son of Duke Bogislaw XIII by his first wife, Clara of Brunswick-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luneburg">Lüneburg</a>. When his father died in 1606 he and his younger brother George II became joint dukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darlowo">Rügenwalde</a>, now Darłowo, and after George died in 1617 Bogislaw ruled it alone. He was also the Lutheran administrator of the Prince-Bishopric of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamien-pomorski">Cammin</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 1620 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darlowo">Rügenwalde</a> was joined to the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/szczecin">Stettin</a>, which Bogislaw inherited on the death of his elder brother Francis. Early in 1625 the last Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolgast">Wolgast</a>, Philipp Julius, died, and Bogislaw became ruler of all West Pomerania. On 19 February of that year he married Elisabeth, a daughter of John II, Duke of Schleswig-Holstein-Sonderburg.</p><p>He tried to keep out of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a>, but in 1627, by the Capitulation of Franzburg, he was forced to let imperial troops under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albrecht-von-wallenstein">Albrecht von Wallenstein</a> use his lands as a base, and the duchy suffered all the consequences of the war. In the early 1630s many of the local nobles worked to reduce his power, and the struggle brought on a stroke that left him partly paralysed. In 1634 he abdicated without a clear successor, which led to a constitutional contest between his relatives and the governing council.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Bogislaw died on 10 March 1637 with the constitutional questions unresolved, no recognised male heir, and nearly all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a> occupied by Swedish and imperial troops. The succession was disputed between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-william-elector-of-brandenburg">George William, Elector of Brandenburg</a>, heir under a pact between the two families of 1464, and King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustavus-adolphus">Gustavus Adolphus</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, whose forces had occupied much of Pomerania after Sweden entered the war. Bogislaw's will left his lands to Sweden, not <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>, if his house had no successor.</p><p>At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-westphalia">Peace of Westphalia</a> in 1648 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a> was divided between the two powers and ceased to be an autonomous political entity. The quarrels over the succession were so grave that his burial was put off for almost 20 years: only after his widow's death was his body laid to rest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/szczecin">Stettin</a>, on 25 May 1654.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bogislaw XIV of Pomerania” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The duchy’s weakness was exposed in the Thirty Years’ War: in 1627 <b>Bogislaw XIV</b> had to accept imperial troops under Albrecht von Wallenstein, who besieged Stralsund in 1628 and failed to take it after the town received Danish and Swedish help.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-pomerania">Duchy of Pomerania · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Bogislaw XIV</b> united both duchies in 1625, but Sweden occupied Pomerania in 1630.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-pomerania-stettin">Duchy of Pomerania-Stettin</a></li><li><blockquote>When Duke Philip Julius died childless in 1625, <b>Bogislaw XIV</b> of Pomerania-Stettin inherited it, uniting Pomerania for the last time shortly before Sweden occupied it in 1630; most of it later became Swedish Pomerania.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-pomerania-wolgast">Duchy of Pomerania-Wolgast</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Barth, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/szczecin">Szczecin</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=54.37,12.73&amp;year=1617" aria-label="Barth on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Bogislaw XIV"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5629 5420h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5629 5420h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=54.37,12.73&amp;year=1617">What was here in 1617? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
